### 5. Extending Normalized Forms to String-Derived Types
- **Presentation:** [07) Extending normalized forms to string-derived types](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-netmod-07-extending-normalized-forms-to-string-derived-types-00) (Scott Mansfield, Don Fedyk)
- **Technical Overview:**
  - Addresses representation mismatches (e.g., uppercase vs. lowercase, hyphen vs. colon) in string-derived types such as MAC addresses between IETF and IEEE definitions.
  - Introduces a model annotation mechanism to specify normalized forms, allowing software to evaluate equivalence across lexical representations.
- **Discussion:**
  - Rob Wilton and Kent Watsen questioned whether this issue extends significantly beyond MAC addresses and noted that RFC 7950 requires servers to return canonical formats. Authors were encouraged to provide full protocol flow examples and solicit broader list feedback.

### 6. Validating anydata in YANG Library Context
- **Presentation:** [08) Validating anydata in YANG Library context](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-netmod-08-validating-anydata-in-yang-library-context-00) (Ahmed Elhassany)
- **Technical Overview:**
  - Discussed mechanisms for verifying YANG data embedded within `anydata` containers.
  - Introduced the concept of a "schema anchor" to specify the exact schema path node from which validation should execute, avoiding full-root validation requirements.
  - Proposed broadening the specification scope beyond YANG Library to support YANG Packages and exploring metadata signals for dynamic schema anchoring. Thomas Graf noted connection to Issue 29 in YANG 2.0.

### 7. YANG Path Format (ypath)
- **Presentation:** [09) YANG path format (ypath)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-netmod-09-yang-path-format-ypath-00) (Rob Wilton on behalf of James Cummings)
- **Technical Overview:**
  - Defines a standardized, light-weight path expression format (`ypath`) using module name prefixes (matching JSON YANG encodings) for schema nodes, instance data nodes, and key-based selection subtrees.
  - Complex predicate filtering and range evaluations remain out of scope for the initial specification.
- **Polls:**
  - **Poll:** *should we be working on ypath? YES = in own /current draft, NO= simple form only in packages, no answer=we should not work on it at all — yes: 19, no: 0, no_opinion: 0 (total: 38)*.
  - **Poll:** *Do you object to working on ypath in own document? — yes: 0, no: 18, no_opinion: 0 (total: 38)*.

### 8. A YANG Extension for Comparability Scope of Operational State
- **Presentation:** [10) A YANG Extension for Comparability Scope of Operational State](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-netmod-10-a-yang-extension-for-comparability-scope-of-operational-state-01) (Sumieh Pathak)
- **Technical Overview:**
  - Proposes a new YANG extension (`comparability-scope`) to define a multi-level hierarchy (`node-local`, `domain-local`, `controller-scope`, `global`) guiding how telemetry collectors compare operational state values across devices.
  - Due to session time constraints, detailed discussion was directed to the mailing list.

